('127.96.d.2.D',3,'Architectural symbols may indicate:','["Phone numbers", "Payroll", "Materials like brick, concrete, and insulation", "Weather"]'::jsonb,2,'Hatch patterns represent materials.','original'),
('127.96.d.2.D',4,'A diagonal hatch with cross lines in a section drawing often shows:','["Water", "Glass", "Sky", "Masonry"]'::jsonb,3,'Pattern fill codes materials.','original'),
('127.96.d.2.E',3,'On plans, the abbreviation ''DR'' usually means:','["Door", "Drive", "Draft", "Drain"]'::jsonb,0,'Abbreviations save space.','original'),
('127.96.d.2.E',4,'The abbreviation ''GYP BD'' stands for:','["Garage plan", "Gypsum board", "Gutter pipe", "Grade piping"]'::jsonb,1,'Gypsum board is drywall.','original'),
('127.96.d.2.F',3,'A wall section is used to show:','["Phone lines", "Parking", "Vertical construction layers of a wall", "Tax records"]'::jsonb,2,'Sections cut through the assembly.','original'),
('127.96.d.2.F',4,'A door schedule lists each door''s:','["Price at store", "Owner''s phone", "Supplier''s hours", "Size, type, and hardware"]'::jsonb,3,'Schedules organize repeated items.','original'),
('127.96.d.2.G',3,'Written specifications describe:','["Materials, quality, and installation requirements", "Employee vacations", "Break times", "Billing hours"]'::jsonb,0,'Specs supplement drawings.','original'),
('127.96.d.2.G',4,'If the drawings and specifications disagree, a builder should:','["Flip a coin", "Ask the architect or supervisor for clarification", "Ignore both", "Pick one"]'::jsonb,1,'Conflicts must be resolved before building.','original'),
('127.96.d.2.H',3,'Specifications are commonly organized into:','["Random pages", "Alphabetical jokes", "Divisions by type of work", "Weather charts"]'::jsonb,2,'CSI divisions organize specs.','original'),
('127.96.d.2.H',4,'A typical specification section includes General, Products, and:','["Entertainment", "Receipts", "Discounts", "Execution"]'::jsonb,3,'Three-part format is standard.','original'),
('127.96.d.2.I',3,'A quantity takeoff is a process of:','["Counting and measuring materials from the plans", "Painting walls", "Hiring workers", "Hanging doors"]'::jsonb,0,'Takeoffs support estimates.','original'),
('127.96.d.2.I',4,'A room is 10 feet by 12 feet. How many square feet of flooring are needed (no waste)?','["22", "120", "240", "44"]'::jsonb,1,'10 x 12 = 120.','original'),
('127.96.d.3.A',3,'A framing square is used to check:','["Voltage", "Temperature", "Right angles", "Weight"]'::jsonb,2,'Squares verify 90 degrees.','original'),
('127.96.d.3.A',4,'A coping saw is used to:','["Measure level", "Drive nails", "Cut sheet metal", "Cut curved profiles on trim for tight joints"]'::jsonb,3,'Coping fits molding over irregular walls.','original'),
('127.96.d.3.B',3,'Keeping a chisel sharp is a safety measure because a sharp chisel:','["Requires less force and is less likely to slip", "Is more dangerous", "Needs more force", "Is quieter"]'::jsonb,0,'Dull tools slip.','original'),
('127.96.d.4.A',3,'A power miter saw is mainly used to make:','["Holes in concrete", "Accurate crosscuts and angled cuts", "Welds", "Curved lines"]'::jsonb,1,'Miter saws cut angles.','original'),
('127.96.d.4.A',4,'A pneumatic nailer is used to:','["Measure lengths", "Cut lumber", "Drive nails quickly with compressed air", "Mix cement"]'::jsonb,2,'Nailers speed fastening.','original'),
('127.96.d.4.B',3,'Before using a power nailer, you should check that the:','["Color matches", "Trigger is taped", "Logo is visible", "Safety contact tip works properly"]'::jsonb,3,'Never disable safety features.','original'),
('127.96.d.4.B',4,'Power tool blades should be:','["Sharp and correct for the material", "Bent", "Dull", "Mismatched"]'::jsonb,0,'Right blade, safe cut.','original'),
('127.96.d.5.A',3,'Newer technologies like laser measures require that users follow:','["No guidelines", "Manufacturer safety instructions", "Rumors", "Guesswork"]'::jsonb,1,'New tools have specific cautions.','original'),
('127.96.d.5.A',4,'Looking directly into a laser beam can:','["Improve sight", "Charge batteries", "Damage the eyes", "Warm the hands"]'::jsonb,2,'Laser safety includes eye protection.','original'),
('127.96.d.5.B',3,'Maintenance on a battery-powered tool includes:','["Soaking it", "Painting it", "Hammering it", "Keeping batteries charged and contacts clean"]'::jsonb,3,'Battery care extends tool life.','original'),
('127.96.d.5.B',4,'Dust buildup in a digital tool''s vents can cause:','["Overheating and failure", "Faster use", "Cooler running", "Longer life"]'::jsonb,0,'Clean tools last.','original'),
('127.96.d.6.A',3,'When replacing a window, a worker should first:','["Remove the whole wall", "Measure the opening and check for hazards", "Skip measuring", "Smash the glass"]'::jsonb,1,'Careful removal and measuring come first.','original'),
('127.96.d.6.A',4,'A shim is used when installing a window to:','["Cut the glass", "Paint trim", "Make the unit plumb and level in the opening", "Insulate"]'::jsonb,2,'Shims adjust fit.','original'),
('127.96.d.6.B',3,'A vapor barrier is installed to:','["Increase noise", "Add weight", "Add color", "Slow moisture movement into building materials"]'::jsonb,3,'Moisture control protects structure.','original'),
('127.96.d.6.B',4,'Building paper or house wrap behind siding helps to:','["Shed water while letting some vapor escape", "Add heat", "Hide studs", "Hold nails only"]'::jsonb,0,'It acts as a water-resistive barrier.','original'),
('127.96.d.7.A',3,'Exterior siding should be installed over:','["Bare air", "A weather-resistive barrier", "Wet carpet", "Open studs only"]'::jsonb,1,'Barriers prevent leaks.','original'),
('127.96.d.7.A',4,'When installing siding, you should leave room for:','["Dust", "Insects", "Expansion and contraction", "Paint"]'::jsonb,2,'Materials move with temperature.','original'),
('127.96.d.7.B',3,'Siding is often fastened with:','["Staples only", "Glue only", "Tape", "Corrosion-resistant nails or screws"]'::jsonb,3,'Exterior fasteners resist rust.','original'),
('127.96.d.7.B',4,'Using the wrong fastener on siding can cause:','["Rust stains and loose boards", "Better fit", "Added strength", "Faster work"]'::jsonb,0,'Match fasteners to the siding.','original'),
('127.96.d.7.C',3,'Stucco is a plaster-like finish applied to:','["Ceilings only", "Exterior walls", "Windows only", "Pipes"]'::jsonb,1,'Stucco is a cement-based exterior finish.','original'),
('127.96.d.7.C',4,'Masonry veneer is a thin layer of:','["Vinyl", "Carpet", "Brick or stone attached to a wall", "Wood paneling"]'::jsonb,2,'Veneer is non-structural.','original'),
('127.96.d.7.D',3,'A common siding type used in many regions is:','["Glass", "Foam only", "Cardboard", "Vinyl"]'::jsonb,3,'Vinyl, wood, and fiber cement are common.','original'),
('127.96.d.7.D',4,'Fiber cement siding is valued for resisting:','["Rot, insects, and fire", "Sound", "Sunlight benefits", "Stains from paint"]'::jsonb,0,'It is durable and low maintenance.','original'),
('127.96.d.8.A',3,'A stud wall is laid out with studs typically spaced:','["3 inches", "16 inches on center", "60 inches", "120 inches"]'::jsonb,1,'16 inches is standard.','original'),
('127.96.d.8.A',4,'A partition wall is one that:','["Is made only of glass", "Cannot have doors", "Divides space but may not carry structural load", "Always holds the roof"]'::jsonb,2,'Partitions separate rooms.','original'),
('127.96.d.8.B',3,'Wood framing is commonly fastened with:','["Tape", "Rope", "Paper clips", "Nails or screws"]'::jsonb,3,'Standard fasteners join wood.','original'),
('127.96.d.8.B',4,'Two 16d nails through the end of a stud into the plate is called:','["End nailing", "Pocket screwing", "Toenailing", "Clinching"]'::jsonb,0,'End nailing fastens through the plate.','original'),
('127.96.d.8.C',3,'Steel studs are usually attached to tracks using:','["Tape", "Self-drilling screws", "Wood nails", "Staples"]'::jsonb,1,'Metal framing uses screws or crimping.','original'),
('127.96.d.8.C',4,'Steel framing is often used in commercial buildings because it is:','["Flammable", "Rot-prone", "Non-combustible and straight", "Warped"]'::jsonb,2,'Steel is stable and fire-safe.','original'),
('127.96.d.9.A',3,'Moisture-resistant drywall is often used in:','["Attics only", "Closets only", "Garages only", "Bathrooms"]'::jsonb,3,'Green board resists moisture.','original'),
('127.96.d.9.A',4,'Fire-rated Type X drywall is thicker and contains:','["Glass fibers", "Sand", "Paint", "Cardboard"]'::jsonb,0,'Type X resists fire longer.','original'),
('127.96.d.9.B',3,'Standard drywall thickness for residential walls is commonly:','["3 inches", "1/2 inch", "2 inches", "1/8 inch"]'::jsonb,1,'1/2 inch is typical.','original'),
('127.96.d.9.B',4,'Ceilings often use 5/8-inch drywall to resist:','["Cold", "Color change", "Sagging", "Noise only"]'::jsonb,2,'Thicker board resists sag.','original'),
('127.96.d.9.C',3,'A fastener schedule tells installers about:','["Colors", "Prices only", "Lunch times", "Screw or nail type and spacing"]'::jsonb,3,'Schedules ensure proper attachment.','original'),
('127.96.d.9.C',4,'Drywall screws should be driven so the head is:','["Slightly below the surface without breaking the paper", "Bent", "Through the board", "Far above the surface"]'::jsonb,0,'Dimpling is correct.','original'),
('127.96.d.9.D',3,'A multi-layer drywall installation is often used for:','["Painting", "Fire or sound control", "Decoration only", "Lighting"]'::jsonb,1,'Extra layers add performance.','original'),
('127.96.d.9.D',4,'Joints in a second layer of drywall are usually:','["Left open", "Painted", "Staggered from the first layer", "Aligned exactly"]'::jsonb,2,'Staggering strengthens the assembly.','original'),
('127.96.d.9.E',3,'Drywall on steel studs is attached with:','["Staples", "Glue only", "Common nails", "Fine-thread screws"]'::jsonb,3,'Fine-thread screws bite metal.','original'),
('127.96.d.9.E',4,'Metal studs are marked with a gauge that indicates:','["Steel thickness", "Length", "Brand", "Color"]'::jsonb,0,'Gauge relates to strength.','original'),
('127.96.d.9.F',3,'A 4-foot by 8-foot sheet of drywall covers how many square feet?','["48", "32", "12", "16"]'::jsonb,1,'4 x 8 = 32.','original'),
('127.96.d.9.F',4,'A wall is 8 feet by 24 feet. About how many 4x8 sheets are needed (no waste)?','["8", "4", "6", "12"]'::jsonb,2,'192 sq ft / 32 = 6.','original'),
('127.96.d.10.A',3,'Drywall finishing levels range from Level 0 to Level:','["20", "10", "2", "5"]'::jsonb,3,'There are six levels, 0 through 5.','original'),
('127.96.d.10.A',4,'Level 5 finish is used when a surface needs a smooth appearance under:','["Critical lighting or gloss paint", "Dark wallpaper", "No finish", "Heavy texture"]'::jsonb,0,'Level 5 adds a skim coat.','original')
