('127.97.d.1.A',3,'During electrical maintenance, the first step before touching wires is to:','["Remove your PPE", "Test with a wet hand", "Leave it energized", "Turn off and lock out the power"]'::jsonb,3,'Lockout/tagout protects workers.','original'),
('127.97.d.1.A',4,'Before working on a light fixture, test the circuit with a:','["Voltage tester", "Hammer", "Tape measure", "Level"]'::jsonb,0,'Verify the power is off.','original'),
('127.97.d.1.B',3,'On a maintenance job, following OSHA rules helps to:','["Avoid training", "Prevent injuries and fines", "Increase hazards", "Cause delays"]'::jsonb,1,'Compliance protects people.','original'),
('127.97.d.1.B',4,'A supervisor can promote on-site safety by:','["Ignoring hazards", "Skipping meetings", "Holding regular safety meetings", "Hiding records"]'::jsonb,2,'Toolbox talks keep safety in mind.','original'),
('127.97.d.1.C',3,'An electrical hazard is a:','["Grounded outlet", "Labeled panel", "Closed junction box", "Frayed extension cord"]'::jsonb,3,'Damaged cords can shock or start fires.','original'),
('127.97.d.1.C',4,'To minimize electrical shock risk near water, use a:','["GFCI-protected circuit", "Wet cord", "Metal ladder near lines", "Bare wire"]'::jsonb,0,'GFCIs shut off power quickly.','original'),
('127.97.d.1.D',3,'Managers must make sure workers have:','["No tools", "Training and appropriate equipment", "No training", "No signs"]'::jsonb,1,'Obligation includes resources.','original'),
('127.97.d.1.D',4,'A worker''s obligation under safety rules includes:','["Ignoring them", "Hiding them", "Reporting unsafe conditions", "Removing warnings"]'::jsonb,2,'Reporting protects everyone.','original'),
('127.97.d.1.E',3,'Accident costs include:','["Extra tools", "Lower pay", "Faster schedules", "Medical care, lost wages, and repairs"]'::jsonb,3,'Costs are direct and indirect.','original'),
('127.97.d.1.E',4,'A near-miss report is useful because it:','["Identifies hazards before someone is hurt", "Hides problems", "Delays work", "Costs nothing"]'::jsonb,0,'Near misses are warnings.','original'),
('127.97.d.1.F',3,'Personal protective equipment for an electrician may include:','["Scarves", "Insulated gloves and safety glasses", "Swim goggles", "Sandals"]'::jsonb,1,'PPE protects against shock.','original'),
('127.97.d.1.F',4,'Hazard communication on chemical containers is done with:','["Nothing", "Smoke signals", "Labels and safety data sheets", "Rumors"]'::jsonb,2,'HazCom standard requires them.','original'),
('127.97.d.10',3,'Historic preservation means:','["Demolishing old buildings", "Painting them neon", "Ignoring them", "Protecting and maintaining significant older buildings"]'::jsonb,3,'Preservation keeps heritage structures.','original'),
('127.97.d.10',4,'Before altering a building in a local historic district, an owner often must get:','["Approval from a historic review board", "No approval", "A fishing license", "A pet permit"]'::jsonb,0,'Local rules guide changes.','original'),
('127.97.d.2.A',3,'A commercial blueprint set usually starts with a:','["Menu", "Cover sheet and index", "Brochure", "Song list"]'::jsonb,1,'The index lists sheets.','original'),
('127.97.d.2.A',4,'Each sheet in a blueprint set has a:','["Picture", "Receipt", "Title block with sheet number", "Phone number"]'::jsonb,2,'Title blocks identify sheets.','original'),
('127.97.d.2.B',3,'Electrical drawings use a symbol for a:','["Window glass", "Roof pitch", "Door color", "Ceiling light"]'::jsonb,3,'Symbols show devices.','original'),
('127.97.d.2.B',4,'Symbols used on commercial drawings are explained in the:','["Legend", "Calendar", "Menu", "Footnote of a magazine"]'::jsonb,0,'Always consult the legend.','original'),
('127.97.d.2.C',3,'An equipment schedule on blueprints lists:','["Vacation dates", "Equipment model, size, and capacity", "Pay rates", "Lunch menu"]'::jsonb,1,'Schedules summarize specs.','original'),
('127.97.d.2.C',4,'A rooftop unit schedule might list the unit''s:','["Owner", "Favorite color", "Cooling capacity and electrical requirements", "Sports team"]'::jsonb,2,'Schedules support installation.','original'),
('127.97.d.3.A',3,'Overcurrent protection devices protect circuits from:','["Too little current", "Rain", "Sunlight", "Too much current"]'::jsonb,3,'Excess current causes overheating.','original'),
('127.97.d.3.A',4,'If a 15-amp circuit carries 30 amps with no breaker, the wires may:','["Overheat and start a fire", "Get lighter", "Work better", "Cool down"]'::jsonb,0,'Overcurrent protection prevents fires.','original'),
('127.97.d.3.B',3,'An ''ampere rating'' on a fuse tells the:','["Color", "Maximum current it can carry", "Price", "Size of the building"]'::jsonb,1,'Ratings define limits.','original'),
('127.97.d.3.B',4,'A fuse ''blows'' when:','["It is wet", "It gets cold", "Current exceeds its rating long enough to melt the element", "It is new"]'::jsonb,2,'Blown fuses open the circuit.','original'),
('127.97.d.3.C',3,'When a circuit breaker trips, it:','["Adds current", "Closes the circuit", "Heats the wire", "Opens the circuit to stop current"]'::jsonb,3,'Breakers can be reset.','original'),
('127.97.d.3.C',4,'Unlike a fuse, a circuit breaker can usually be:','["Reset after the fault is fixed", "Eaten", "Used once", "Thrown out every time"]'::jsonb,0,'Breakers are reusable.','original'),
('127.97.d.3.D',3,'A time-delay fuse allows:','["All surges forever", "Brief surges such as motor starting", "No current", "No surge"]'::jsonb,1,'Time-delay fuses tolerate inrush.','original'),
('127.97.d.3.D',4,'A single-element fuse is best used on circuits where there are:','["Large motors", "Compressors", "No large startup surges", "Pumps"]'::jsonb,2,'Single-element fuses react fast.','original'),
('127.97.d.3.E',3,'A GFCI protects people by cutting power when it senses:','["Low voltage", "A bright light", "Cool air", "Current leaking to ground"]'::jsonb,3,'GFCIs trip at about 5 milliamps.','original'),
('127.97.d.3.E',4,'GFCIs are required by code in locations such as:','["Bathrooms and kitchens near water", "Closets", "Attics", "Hallways"]'::jsonb,0,'Wet locations need GFCI protection.','original'),
('127.97.d.3.F',3,'If a breaker trips repeatedly, the electrician should:','["Install a larger breaker without checking", "Find the cause of the overload or fault", "Tape the breaker on", "Ignore it"]'::jsonb,1,'Repeated trips signal a problem.','original'),
('127.97.d.3.F',4,'Maintenance on panels should include checking for:','["Stickers", "New paint", "Loose connections and signs of heat", "Dust bunnies"]'::jsonb,2,'Heat marks reveal problems.','original'),
('127.97.d.4.A',3,'An LED lamp is known for:','["High heat", "Short life", "High energy use", "Low energy use and long life"]'::jsonb,3,'LEDs are efficient.','original'),
('127.97.d.4.A',4,'An incandescent bulb wastes most of its energy as:','["Heat", "Sparks", "Water", "Sound"]'::jsonb,0,'Incandescent lamps are inefficient.','original'),
('127.97.d.4.B',3,'Before replacing a lamp in a fixture, you should:','["Skip the switch", "Turn the power off", "Hold the switch on", "Use wet hands"]'::jsonb,1,'Safe practice.','original'),
('127.97.d.4.B',4,'Using a lamp with too high a wattage can:','["Cool the room", "Fix wiring", "Overheat the fixture", "Save energy"]'::jsonb,2,'Respect fixture ratings.','original'),
('127.97.d.4.C',3,'A recessed light fixture is installed:','["On the floor", "In a wall switch box", "On a roof", "In an opening cut in the ceiling"]'::jsonb,3,'Recessed fixtures sit in the ceiling.','original'),
('127.97.d.4.C',4,'A pendant fixture hangs from the ceiling by a:','["Cord, chain, or stem", "Wall paint", "Magnet only", "Tape"]'::jsonb,0,'Pendants hang.','original'),
('127.97.d.5.A',3,'A multimeter can be used to measure:','["Temperature of wood", "Voltage, current, and resistance", "Sound only", "Weight only"]'::jsonb,1,'Multimeters are versatile.','original'),
('127.97.d.5.A',4,'A clamp-on ammeter measures current without:','["Wearing PPE", "Plugging into a wall", "Disconnecting the wire", "Calibrating"]'::jsonb,2,'Clamp meters sense magnetic fields.','original'),
('127.97.d.5.B',3,'To read a meter correctly, you must first set the:','["Clock", "Name tag", "Color", "Function and range"]'::jsonb,3,'Wrong setting gives wrong readings or damage.','original'),
('127.97.d.5.B',4,'If a meter reads 0.5 on the ''kilo'' scale, the reading is:','["500 units", "0.5 units", "50 units", "5 units"]'::jsonb,0,'Kilo means thousand.','original'),
('127.97.d.5.C',3,'Meter polarity matters when measuring DC because:','["It adds voltage", "Reversing leads gives a negative reading or may damage a meter", "It changes color", "It cleans the circuit"]'::jsonb,1,'Red positive, black negative.','original'),
('127.97.d.5.C',4,'On a DC voltage test, the red lead goes to the:','["Ground only", "Neutral only", "Positive side of the circuit", "Negative side"]'::jsonb,2,'Follow the polarity.','original'),
('127.97.d.5.D',3,'Frequency is measured in:','["Ohms", "Volts", "Amps", "Hertz"]'::jsonb,3,'One Hz is one cycle per second.','original'),
('127.97.d.5.D',4,'In the U.S., standard AC power frequency is:','["60 hertz", "10 hertz", "200 hertz", "1 hertz"]'::jsonb,0,'North America uses 60 Hz.','original'),
('127.97.d.5.E',3,'A digital meter displays the reading as:','["A flag", "Numbers", "A dial only", "A needle"]'::jsonb,1,'Digital meters show digits.','original'),
('127.97.d.5.E',4,'An analog meter shows readings with a:','["Light only", "Beep only", "Moving needle on a scale", "Display of numbers"]'::jsonb,2,'Analog uses a pointer.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.A',3,'Electrical service requirements for a house depend on the:','["Street name", "Paint color", "Number of windows only", "Total expected electrical load"]'::jsonb,3,'Load calculations size the service.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.A',4,'A common residential service size today is:','["200 amps", "2000 amps", "2 amps", "5 amps"]'::jsonb,0,'200 A is typical for new homes.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.B',3,'Wiring methods include:','["String", "Nonmetallic cable and conduit", "Tape", "Rope"]'::jsonb,1,'Methods depend on location and code.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.B',4,'Conduit is used to:','["Hide leaks", "Hold water", "Protect and route electrical wires", "Support roofs"]'::jsonb,2,'Conduit shields wiring.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'Career Development' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
insert into public.question_bank (teks_code, teks_item_id, subject, grade_num, course, difficulty, qtype, prompt, choices, answer_idx, explanation, source, status, gen_batch)
select distinct on (v.prompt) v.code, i.id, d.subject,
       case when i.grade_level = 'K' then 0 when i.grade_level ~ '^[0-9]+' then substring(i.grade_level from '^[0-9]+')::smallint end,
       case when i.grade_level is null then i.grade_course_title end,
       v.diff, 'mc', v.prompt, v.choices, v.ans, v.expl, v.src, 'checked', 'cte_13_v1'
from (values
('127.97.d.6.C',3,'The National Electrical Code (NEC) sets rules for:','["Landscaping", "Roof pitch", "Plumbing", "Safe electrical installations"]'::jsonb,3,'The NEC is updated every three years.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.C',4,'The NEC becomes enforceable in a city when it is:','["Adopted by local authorities", "Mentioned online", "Printed", "Advertised"]'::jsonb,0,'Local adoption makes it law.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.D',3,'A branch circuit load is the total power drawn by:','["The panel label", "Devices connected to that circuit", "The gas line", "The entire neighborhood"]'::jsonb,1,'Branch circuits serve outlets and loads.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.D',4,'A 120-volt circuit that supplies 1,200 watts draws how many amps?','["1", "20", "10", "100"]'::jsonb,2,'Amps = watts / volts = 1200/120 = 10.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.E',3,'An equipment grounding conductor provides a path for:','["Heat", "Light", "Water", "Fault current to return safely"]'::jsonb,3,'Grounding trips breakers.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.E',4,'The bare or green wire in a cable is generally the:','["Equipment ground", "Switch leg", "Neutral", "Hot wire"]'::jsonb,0,'Ground wires are bare or green.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.F',3,'An outlet box size is chosen based on the number of:','["Windows", "Conductors, devices, and fittings inside", "Bricks", "Floors"]'::jsonb,1,'Box fill rules apply.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.F',4,'Overcrowding an outlet box can cause:','["Lower bills", "Better connections", "Overheating and damaged insulation", "Cooler wires"]'::jsonb,2,'Box fill protects wires.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.G',3,'Electric space heaters installed permanently must be:','["Hung from cords", "Left uncovered", "Plugged into powerstrips", "Wired according to code and on their own circuits"]'::jsonb,3,'Heaters draw high current.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.G',4,'HVAC equipment requires a dedicated circuit to avoid:','["Overloading shared circuits", "Bright lights", "Lower cost", "Quiet running"]'::jsonb,0,'Dedicated circuits support heavy loads.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.H',3,'Electrical wiring near a swimming pool requires special:','["Paint", "Grounding, bonding, and GFCI protection", "Music", "Decorations"]'::jsonb,1,'Water increases shock risk.','original'),
('127.97.d.6.H',4,'Equipotential bonding around a pool is used to:','["Brighten lights", "Lower bills", "Keep metal parts at the same voltage", "Heat the water"]'::jsonb,2,'Bonding prevents shock gradients.','original'),
('127.97.d.7.A',3,'An air conditioner removes heat from inside a building by using:','["Sunlight", "Water only", "Wind", "Refrigerant that absorbs and releases heat"]'::jsonb,3,'The refrigeration cycle moves heat.','original'),
('127.97.d.7.A',4,'The four main components of a basic refrigeration cycle are the compressor, condenser, metering device, and:','["Evaporator", "Radiator", "Humidifier", "Generator"]'::jsonb,0,'The evaporator absorbs heat.','original'),
('127.97.d.7.B',3,'The Clean Air Act affects HVAC by regulating:','["Carpet", "Refrigerants and their release", "Paint", "Doors"]'::jsonb,1,'Venting refrigerants is prohibited.','original'),
('127.97.d.7.B',4,'EPA Section 608 certification is needed to:','["Drive trucks", "Paint walls", "Handle refrigerants", "Hang doors"]'::jsonb,2,'Technicians must be certified.','original'),
('127.97.d.7.C',3,'HVAC drawings often include:','["Menus", "Tax forms", "Recipes", "Duct layouts and equipment schedules"]'::jsonb,3,'They guide installation.','original'),
('127.97.d.7.C',4,'A mechanical schedule lists equipment such as:','["Air handlers and their capacities", "Trim", "Windows", "Doors"]'::jsonb,0,'Schedules provide technical data.','original'),
('127.97.d.8.A',3,'When installing refrigerant piping, precautions include:','["Skipping tests", "Using clean, dry tubing and avoiding leaks", "Using dirty tubing", "Leaving it open"]'::jsonb,1,'Contamination harms systems.','original'),
('127.97.d.8.A',4,'Wear safety glasses and gloves when brazing or handling refrigerant to guard against:','["Hearing loss", "Cramps", "Burns and frostbite", "Sunburn"]'::jsonb,2,'Refrigerant can freeze skin.','original'),
('127.97.d.8.B',3,'Refrigeration systems typically use tubing made of:','["Paper", "Rubber bands", "Plastic bags", "Copper"]'::jsonb,3,'Copper conducts heat and resists corrosion.','original'),
('127.97.d.8.B',4,'Tubing size is chosen based on the system''s:','["Capacity and refrigerant flow needs", "Price of lunch", "Color", "Trim style"]'::jsonb,0,'Wrong size hurts performance.','original'),
('127.97.d.8.C',3,'Bending copper tubing is done with a:','["Hammer only", "Tube bender", "Wrench only", "Torch only"]'::jsonb,1,'Benders prevent kinks.','original'),
('127.97.d.8.C',4,'A tubing cutter produces a:','["Flat cut with burrs", "Rough saw cut", "Clean, square cut", "Angled cut"]'::jsonb,2,'Ream after cutting.','original'),
('127.97.d.8.D',3,'Refrigerant lines are supported with hangers to prevent:','["Freezing", "Cooling", "Heating", "Sagging and vibration"]'::jsonb,3,'Supports protect piping.','original'),
('127.97.d.8.D',4,'Pipe supports should be spaced according to:','["Code and manufacturer recommendations", "Color", "Whim", "Mood"]'::jsonb,0,'Spacing depends on pipe size.','original'),
('127.97.d.8.E',3,'After installing refrigerant lines, the system is pressure-tested using:','["Natural gas", "Dry nitrogen", "Oxygen", "Water only"]'::jsonb,1,'Nitrogen is safe and inert.','original'),
('127.97.d.8.E',4,'Pressure testing is done to find:','["Colors", "Dust", "Leaks before charging", "Wrinkles"]'::jsonb,2,'Leak-free systems operate efficiently.','original'),
('127.97.d.8.F',3,'A common plastic pipe used for condensate drains is:','["Steel", "Copper", "Brass", "PVC"]'::jsonb,3,'PVC is common for drains.','original'),
('127.97.d.8.F',4,'PVC pipe is suitable for:','["Drain lines and cold water, not high heat", "Gas furnaces", "Refrigerant lines", "Boilers"]'::jsonb,0,'Know material limits.','original'),
('127.97.d.8.G',3,'PVC pipe is joined using:','["Tape", "Primer and solvent cement", "Staples", "Nails"]'::jsonb,1,'Solvent welding makes a bond.','original'),
('127.97.d.8.G',4,'Before gluing PVC, the pipe end should be:','["Wet", "Painted", "Cut square and deburred", "Left rough"]'::jsonb,2,'Prep ensures a tight joint.','original'),
('127.97.d.9.A',3,'A thermostat controls a heating or cooling system by:','["Painting walls", "Playing music", "Counting people", "Sensing room temperature and signaling equipment"]'::jsonb,3,'Thermostats cycle equipment.','original'),
('127.97.d.9.A',4,'A programmable thermostat saves energy by:','["Adjusting temperature on a schedule", "Running constantly", "Increasing use", "Staying fixed"]'::jsonb,0,'Programming reduces use when away.','original'),
('127.97.d.9.B',3,'Pneumatic controls use:','["Sunlight", "Compressed air signals", "Sound waves", "Water signals"]'::jsonb,1,'Air pressure signals actuators.','original'),
('127.97.d.9.B',4,'Electronic controls differ from pneumatic ones because they use:','["Hydraulics", "Air pressure", "Electrical signals", "Springs only"]'::jsonb,2,'Electronic circuits process signals.','original'),
('127.97.d.9.C',3,'A control circuit diagram shows:','["Dimensions of doors", "Wall depths", "Paint colors", "How components are connected in a control system"]'::jsonb,3,'Diagrams guide troubleshooting.','original'),
('127.97.d.9.C',4,'A microprocessor-based controller can:','["Store settings and run programmed logic", "Cut wires", "Hold water", "Hang pictures"]'::jsonb,0,'Programs automate control.','original'),
('127.97.d.9.D',3,'When a system is not heating, the technician starts troubleshooting by:','["Replacing everything", "Checking power, thermostat, and basic controls", "Ignoring the complaint", "Guessing"]'::jsonb,1,'Start with simple checks.','original'),
('127.97.d.9.D',4,'A tool for checking whether a control circuit has voltage is a:','["Level", "Chisel", "Multimeter", "Hammer"]'::jsonb,2,'Meters verify power.','original'),
('127.96.d.3.B',4,'A maintenance carpenter notices a hand plane iron is chipped. The best action is to:','["Hit it with a hammer", "Use it anyway", "Cover it with tape", "Regrind or replace the iron before using it"]'::jsonb,3,'A damaged edge tears wood and can slip.','original')
